\title{A Generalized Multinomial Convolution Identity\\ for Latin Rectangle Enumeration}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We prove a general convolution identity for products of binomial coefficients indexed by ordered compositions. For all non-negative integers $m \ge 1$, $a$, and $d$,
\[
\sum_{i_1+\cdots+i_m=d}\;\prod_{j=1}^{m}\binom{a+i_j}{a} \;=\; \binom{ma+d+m-1}{d},
\]
where the sum ranges over all ordered $m$-tuples of non-negative integers summing to $d$. The case $m=3$ is the identity used to simplify the Bogart--Longyear closed form for the number of three-row Latin rectangles, where it was described only as being ``easily proved with dots and dividers.'' We give a complete, self-contained proof: a two-factor \emph{negative binomial convolution} serves as the base engine, a coordinate-splitting recursion reduces an $m$-tuple sum to nested two-factor convolutions, and induction on the number of factors closes the identity. We record the specializations $m=2$ and $m=3$, a direct two-factor form over the ordinary antidiagonal, and the stars-and-bars count recovered at $a=0$. We conclude with the generating-function interpretation that makes the identity transparent and outline extensions to mixed parameters, multisymmetric-function series, and $q$-analogues.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} binomial convolution, negative binomial series, compositions, stars and bars, Latin rectangles, Vandermonde-type identities, generating functions.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{MSC 2020:} 05A10, 05A19, 05B15, 05A15.

\section{Introduction}

A \emph{Latin rectangle} of size $k \times n$ is a $k \times n$ array of symbols from an $n$-element alphabet such that no symbol repeats within any row or any column. Enumerating Latin rectangles is a central and difficult problem of combinatorial design theory, with connections to experimental design, coding theory, and the permanent of $0/1$ matrices. Even the three-row case is delicate: the classical closed form of Bogart and Longyear for the number of $3 \times n$ Latin rectangles is expressed as a sum of products of binomial coefficients.

Within the derivation of that closed form, a subordinate identity appears whose role is purely to collapse an inclusion--exclusion sum into a single binomial coefficient. In the original treatment this identity is dispatched with the phrase ``easily proved with dots and dividers,'' a reference to the stars-and-bars technique of elementary enumerative combinatorics. In the three-factor form relevant there, the identity reads
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i+j+k=d}\binom{a+i}{a}\binom{a+j}{a}\binom{a+k}{a} \;=\; \binom{3a+d+2}{d}. \label{eq:three}
\end{equation}

The purpose of this paper is to prove, in full and for arbitrary numbers of factors, the natural generalization of \eqref{eq:three}:
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i_1+\cdots+i_m=d}\;\prod_{j=1}^{m}\binom{a+i_j}{a} \;=\; \binom{ma+d+m-1}{d}, \qquad m \ge 1. \label{eq:main}
\end{equation}

Identity \eqref{eq:main} is a member of the classical family of ``negative binomial'' or ``Vandermonde-type'' convolutions, and it admits a transparent generating-function reading: the sequence $i \mapsto \binom{a+i}{a}$ is the coefficient sequence of $(1-x)^{-(a+1)}$, so \eqref{eq:main} is the statement that the product of $m$ copies of $(1-x)^{-(a+1)}$ equals $(1-x)^{-m(a+1)}$, whose $x^d$-coefficient is $\binom{m(a+1)-1+d}{d} = \binom{ma+m-1+d}{d}$. Our contribution is a fully rigorous, elementary proof using a single two-factor convolution lemma, a coordinate-splitting recursion for tuple sums, and induction.

\section{Definitions and notation}

Throughout, $\N = \{0,1,2,\dots\}$ and all variables are non-negative integers. We write $\binom{n}{k}$ for the ordinary binomial coefficient, with $\binom{n}{k}=0$ when $k>n$.

\begin{definition}[Ordinary antidiagonal]
For $d \in \N$, the \emph{antidiagonal} of $d$ is $A(d) = \{(i,j)\in\N^2 : i+j=d\}$, which has $d+1$ elements.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Ordered $m$-tuple compositions]
For $m,d \in \N$, the \emph{antidiagonal tuple set} $T(m,d)$ is the set of ordered $m$-tuples $(x_1,\dots,x_m)$ of non-negative integers with $\sum_{j=1}^m x_j = d$. These are the weak compositions of $d$ into $m$ parts, and $|T(m,d)| = \binom{d+m-1}{d}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Negative binomial weight]
For fixed $a\in\N$, the sequence $c_a(i)=\binom{a+i}{a}=\binom{a+i}{i}$ counts the multisets of size $i$ from an $(a+1)$-element alphabet, and is the coefficient of $x^i$ in $(1-x)^{-(a+1)}$.
\end{definition}

The object of study is the weighted tuple sum
\[
S_m(a,d) = \sum_{x \in T(m,d)}\prod_{j=1}^{m} c_a(x_j) = \sum_{x_1+\cdots+x_m=d}\prod_{j=1}^m\binom{a+x_j}{a}.
\]

\section{The two-factor negative binomial convolution}

\begin{theorem}[Negative binomial convolution]\label{thm:two}
For all $p,q,d \in \N$,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i+j=d}\binom{p+i}{p}\binom{q+j}{q} \;=\; \binom{p+q+1+d}{d}. \label{eq:conv2}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Double induction. When $q=0$, the factor $\binom{q+j}{q}=1$, so the left side is $\sum_{i=0}^{d}\binom{p+i}{p}$, which equals $\binom{p+d+1}{d}$ by the hockey-stick identity (itself an induction on $d$ using Pascal's rule and the symmetry $\binom{n}{k}=\binom{n}{n-k}$). For the step, assume \eqref{eq:conv2} for $q$; apply Pascal's rule $\binom{q+1+j}{q+1}=\binom{q+j}{q+1}+\binom{q+j}{q}$ and split the sum. Reindexing the antidiagonal by the successor map $(i,j)\mapsto(i,j+1)$ reduces one summand to the induction hypothesis in $q$; an inner induction on $d$ handles the other. Regrouping via Pascal's rule and the symmetry relation collapses both contributions to $\binom{p+q+2+d}{d}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Identity \eqref{eq:conv2} is the Vandermonde/Chu convolution in negative-upper-index form: via $\binom{p+i}{p}=(-1)^i\binom{-(p+1)}{i}$ it becomes $\sum_{i+j=d}\binom{-(p+1)}{i}\binom{-(q+1)}{j}=\binom{-(p+q+2)}{d}$.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Equal-parameter two-factor form]\label{cor:two-eq}
For all $a,d\in\N$, $\displaystyle\sum_{i+j=d}\binom{a+i}{a}\binom{a+j}{a}=\binom{2a+d+1}{d}.$
\end{corollary}

\section{The coordinate-splitting recursion}

For an $(k+1)$-tuple $x=(x_0,x_1,\dots,x_k)$ write $x=(x_0,y)$ with $y=(x_1,\dots,x_k)$, and let $\cons(t,y)$ prepend $t$ to the $k$-tuple $y$.

\begin{lemma}[Coordinate splitting]\label{lem:split}
Let $M$ be a commutative monoid and $f:\N^{k+1}\to M$. Then
\begin{equation}
\sum_{x \in T(k+1,\,n)} f(x) \;=\; \sum_{(t,r)\in A(n)}\;\sum_{y \in T(k,\,r)} f(\cons(t,y)). \label{eq:split}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The map $x\mapsto\bigl((x_0,n-x_0),(x_1,\dots,x_k)\bigr)$ is a bijection from $T(k+1,n)$ onto $\bigsqcup_{(t,r)\in A(n)}T(k,r)$, with inverse $((t,r),y)\mapsto\cons(t,y)$. Transporting $f$ term by term gives \eqref{eq:split}.
\end{proof}

\section{The generalized multinomial convolution identity}

\begin{theorem}[Main identity, $m+1$ factors]\label{thm:main1}
For all $m,a,d\in\N$,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{x \in T(m+1,\,d)}\;\prod_{j=0}^{m}\binom{a+x_j}{a} \;=\; \binom{(m+1)a + d + m}{d}. \label{eq:mainplus}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $m$. For $m=0$, $T(1,d)=\{(d)\}$ and the left side is $\binom{a+d}{a}=\binom{a+d}{d}$, matching the right side. Assume \eqref{eq:mainplus} for $m$ and consider $(m+2)$-tuples. By Lemma~\ref{lem:split} with $k=m+1$, $n=d$, $f(x)=\prod_{j=0}^{m+1}\binom{a+x_j}{a}$,
\[
\sum_{x\in T(m+2,d)}f(x)=\sum_{(t,r)\in A(d)}\binom{a+t}{a}\sum_{y\in T(m+1,r)}\prod_{j=0}^m\binom{a+y_j}{a}.
\]
By the induction hypothesis the inner sum is $\binom{(m+1)a+r+m}{r}=\binom{b+r}{b}$ with $b=(m+1)a+m$. Hence the total is $\sum_{(t,r)\in A(d)}\binom{a+t}{a}\binom{b+r}{b}$, a two-factor convolution. Theorem~\ref{thm:two} gives $\binom{a+b+1+d}{d}=\binom{(m+2)a+d+(m+1)}{d}$, completing the induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Main identity, stated form]\label{thm:main}
For all $a,d\in\N$ and every $m\ge1$,
\[
\sum_{x \in T(m,\,d)}\;\prod_{j=1}^{m}\binom{a+x_j}{a} \;=\; \binom{ma + d + m - 1}{d}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $m=m'+1$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:main1}: the right side is $\binom{(m'+1)a+d+m'}{d}=\binom{ma+d+m-1}{d}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[$m=3$, Bogart--Longyear identity]
$\displaystyle\sum_{i+j+k=d}\binom{a+i}{a}\binom{a+j}{a}\binom{a+k}{a}=\binom{3a+d+2}{d}.$
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Stars and bars, $a=0$]
For all $m\ge1$, $d\ge0$, $\;|T(m,d)|=\binom{d+m-1}{d}.$
\end{corollary}

\section{Generating-function interpretation}

Recall the negative binomial series
\[
\frac{1}{(1-x)^{a+1}}=\sum_{i=0}^\infty\binom{a+i}{a}x^i.
\]
The Cauchy product realizes convolution: $[x^d]\,F(x)G(x)=\sum_{i+j=d}f_ig_j$. Applying this to $m$ copies,
\[
\left(\frac{1}{(1-x)^{a+1}}\right)^{\!m}=\frac{1}{(1-x)^{m(a+1)}}=\sum_{d=0}^\infty\binom{ma+m-1+d}{d}x^d,
\]
and reading off the coefficient of $x^d$ yields the main identity. Thus the identity is precisely the additivity of exponents under multiplication, and the inductive proof is a term-level unwinding of that fact.

\section{Application to Latin rectangle enumeration}

The number of $k\times n$ Latin rectangles is governed, for small $k$, by inclusion--exclusion over forbidden column patterns. For $k=3$, the Bogart--Longyear analysis produces sums of the shape \eqref{eq:three} when column contributions are grouped by type; replacing each by $\binom{3a+d+2}{d}$ collapses a double summation into a single one. The generalization \eqref{eq:main} makes this mechanism uniform across the number of factors, positioning the identity as a reusable simplification lemma rather than an ad hoc trick, and connecting it, through the generating-function reading, to the multisymmetric-function series framework.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Mixed parameters.} Since Theorem~\ref{thm:two} carries independent $p,q$, the same induction proves $\sum_{i_1+\cdots+i_m=d}\prod_j\binom{a_j+i_j}{a_j}=\binom{(\sum a_j)+m-1+d}{d}$.
\item \textbf{Generating-function bridge.} Packaging Theorem~\ref{thm:two} as $(1-x)^{-(p+1)}(1-x)^{-(q+1)}=(1-x)^{-(p+q+2)}$ gives a second, structural proof.
\item \textbf{Bogart--Longyear simplification.} Carry the $m=3$ case through the full three-row count and relate the $m$-fold identity to $m$-row generating series.
\item \textbf{Multiset formulation.} Recast the ordered-tuple sum as a multiset sum with multiplicity weights, matching symmetric-function series.
\item \textbf{$q$-analogue.} A Gaussian version holds after appropriate $q$-power weighting; the precise refined identity is worth pinning down.
\end{enumerate}

